    @Component
    class ServiceManager implements ApplicationRunner {
        private final Random random = new Random();

        @Override
        public void run(ApplicationArguments args) throws Exception {
            while (true) {
                serviceExecutor.submit(() -> startService());
                Thread.sleep(random.nextInt(10000)); // Adjust the delay to control frequency
            }
        }
    }

    @Component
    class ServiceController {
        private final static String SERVICE_TOPIC = "service-topic";

        @KafkaListener(topics = SERVICE_TOPIC)
        public void receiveMessage(String message) {
            if ("stop".equalsIgnoreCase(message)) {
                stopService();
                streamBridge.send("monitoring-topic", "service stopped");
            }
        }
    }

    private void startService() {
        // Start the service implementation here
        LOGGER.info("Starting service...");
        // Implement health checks and monitoring, concurrency controls, fault tolerance strategies, etc.
    }

    private void stopService() {
        // Stop the service implementation here
        LOGGER.info("Stopping service...");
        serviceExecutor.shutdown();
        try {
            serviceExecutor.awaitTermination(10, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            LOGGER.error("Error while waiting for the service termination", e);
        }
    }
}
```

This example demonstrates a basic service lifecycle manager using Spring Boot and Kafka. The manager periodically starts and stops the service, and sends messages to a monitoring system when the service is stopped. The service implementation should be added in the `startService()` method, with health checks, concurrency controls, fault tolerance strategies, and other requirements integrated as needed.

For more advanced features like horizontal scaling, intelligent load balancing, and dynamic resource allocation, you can consider using container orchestration tools such as Kubernetes and service mesh solutions like Istio. To improve performance, use appropriate data structures and algorithms for message queueing and data synchronization. For handling network latencies, consider implementing retry logic and backoff strategies. Lastly, to mitigate the impact of external system failures, you can implement circuit breakers or bulkhead isolation patterns.']

