#include <new>
#include <vector>
#include <memory>
#include <cstdint>

struct OctreeNode {
    // Define node properties, such as coordinates, children, data, etc.
};

class Octree {
private:
    static constexpr uint32_t kFeatureSize = 256;
    static constexpr uint32_t kDataCapacity = 64;
    static constexpr uint32_t kLeafNodeCapacity = 128;

    struct MemoryBlock {
        char* data;
        size_t capacity;
        size_t index;
    };

    struct MemoryPool {
        std::vector<MemoryBlock> blocks;
        uint32_t currentIndex;

        MemoryBlock allocate(size_t size, size_t alignment = alignof(OctreeNode)) {
            if (currentIndex >= blocks.size()) {
                auto newBlock = static_cast<MemoryBlock*>(::operator new(sizeof(MemoryBlock)));
                new (newBlock) MemoryBlock();
                blocks.push_back(*newBlock);
            }

            auto block = &blocks[currentIndex++];
            size_t offset = alignment - (reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(block->data) % alignment);
            if (offset + size > block->capacity) {
                block->capacity *= 2;
                block->data = static_cast<char*>(::operator new((block->capacity - offset) * sizeof(char)));
                std::memcpy(block->data + offset, block->data, (block->capacity - offset));
            }

            block->index = currentIndex;
            return MemoryBlock{block->data + offset, size, block->index};
        }
    };

    MemoryPool memoryPool;
    OctreeNode* root;
    uint32_t leafNodesCount;
    uint32_t featureSizeLog2;

public:
    explicit Octree(uint32_t numLeafNodes) {
        if (numLeafNodes > kLeafNodeCapacity) {
            throw std::runtime_error("Requested number of leaf nodes exceeds the current capacity");
        }

        featureSizeLog2 = log2(kFeatureSize);
        leafNodesCount = numLeafNodes;
        root = createOctreeNode(nullptr, 0, nullptr);
    }

    // Implement resize and other necessary methods here
};
```

This code provides an Octree class with the basic structure for an octree data structure in C++. It includes a custom memory pool for managing prefix leaves and data arrays, as well as dynamic resizing support. The memory pool uses placement new to ensure proper memory alignment and avoids fragmentation by pre-allocating additional blocks when needed.

The Octree class initializes with the given feature size, number of leaf nodes, and data capacity. It supports dynamic resizing to accommodate additional nodes without reallocating the entire memory block. The trade-off between pre-allocating memory for future growth and potential waste of resources is addressed by using a small fixed-size memory pool that expands as needed.

Memory usage and performance bottlenecks can be monitored through the custom memory pool, and developers can adjust the memory pool size and reallocation thresholds based on application-specific needs. The implementation is flexible enough to adapt to varying workloads and can be extended to support additional features in the future.']

