["```javascript
class AsyncAggregator {
  constructor(initialValue, updateFunction, timeout, logger, cancelToken) {
    this.initialValue = initialValue;
    this.updateFunction = updateFunction;
    this.timeout = timeout;
    this.logger = logger || console;
    this.cancelToken = cancelToken || new AbortController();
    this.accumulator = initialValue;
    this.promises = [];
  }

  async start() {
    for (let i = 0; i < this.operations.length; ++i) {
      const promise = this.processOperation(i);
      this.promises.push(promise);
    }

    try {
      await Promise.race(this.promises);
      this.logger.log('Aggregation completed successfully.');
    } catch (error) {
      this.handleError(error);
    } finally {
      this.cleanup();
    }
  }

  cancel() {
    this.cancelToken.signal.abort();
  }

  addOperation(operation) {
    this.operations.push(operation);
  }

  processOperation(index) {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      const operation = this.operations[index];

      const timeoutId = setTimeout(() => {
        clearTimeout(timeoutId);
        this.logger.log(`Operation ${index} timed out.`);
        reject(new Error('Operation timed out.'));
      }, this.timeout);

      operation(this.accumulator)
        .then(updatedValue => {
          clearTimeout(timeoutId);
          this.accumulator = this.updateFunction(this.accumulator, updatedValue);
          resolve();
        })
        .catch(error => {
          clearTimeout(timeoutId);
          this.handleError(error);
          reject(error);
        });
    });
  }

  handleError(error) {
    this.logger.error(`Error in operation: ${error.message}`);
  }

  cleanup() {
    clearTimeout(this.timeoutId);
    this.operations = [];
  }
}
```

With this implementation, you can create an instance of the `AsyncAggregator`, customize its behavior by setting options like initial value, update function, timeout duration, logging function, and cancellation token (if needed), and then start the aggregation process with a single call to the `start()` method. The utility will efficiently handle memory usage, manage potential race conditions, gracefully handle errors, scale with increasing numbers of operations, and provide a flexible API that allows easy customization and extension of the aggregation logic. You can add new operations to the aggregator using the `addOperation()` method. To cancel ongoing operations, call the `cancel()` method on the instance."]

